  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 248 PM MDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nSynoptically, very little has changed for Sunday through Thursday. \nFor the majority of this period, our upper level flow is forecast to \nremain from the northwest. Near the Four Corners region, an upper \nlevel ridge is expected to stay near stationary as various troughs \nmove troughs move along the US/Canada border with the jet stream. \nGuidance continues to indicate that a shortwave trough could move \nthrough the Tri-State Area around Tuesday and Wednesday. However, \nthere is still some uncertainty regarding the exact track and \nstrength of the shortwave.\n\nHigh temperatures for the week are currently forecast to be in the \n80s for the majority of the period. Currently, the coolest day is \nforecast to be Sunday, with highs in the low to mid 70s. The warmest \nday is forecast to be Tuesday with highs in the 80s across most of \nthe County Warning Area (CWA). Precipitation chances for this period \nremain quite low, with Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) around 5-\n10% each day. However, if the potential shortwave moving through on \nTuesday/Wednesday continues to intensify, there could be a slight \nincrease in PoPs. \n\nWinds for the period look to remain mainly under 20 mph, with gusts \nunder 25 mph out of the south. Monday afternoon still appears to be \nthe windiest day, with sustained southernly winds around 10-20 mph \nand gusts reaching 25-35 mph. Looking at Deterministic guidance, the \nCanadian-NH, ECMWF, and GFS all show an 850 mb jet set up over the \nTri-State area oriented south to north, however each model have the \njet at different intensities. Ensemble guidance remains in slight \ndisagreement on where the highest gusts will be seen. The LREF \nfavors more widespread gusts of 25-30 mph across the CWA, where as \nthe NBM has the gusts of 25-35 mph primarily west of Hwy 83 and north \nof Hwy 40. Fire weather concerns remain low for the CWA, as relative \nhumidity values generally stay above 25% on Monday and recent \nprecipitation will likely keep soil moisture fairly saturated.",
